WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.860
[Multiple gun shots]

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.020
[Electric guitar playing]

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:13.600
[Niko:] Oh, hey, what's up again?

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:14.540
[Laughing]

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.980
Ugh, we've been working on this video for

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.360
like non-stop two weeks now

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.200
Most of our videos that we do with our brand intergrations

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.740
We have plenty of time to turn them around, well, not plenty - enough.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.740
This PUBG video in particular; we have three weeks shooting it, to finishing it

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.860
We need to finish it early

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.540
We need to finish it in time to send off

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.860
The companies time to look at the video. Give us the thumbs up

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.020
That means being really on top of things and the only way we're gonna get this done

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.660
is to actually coordinate the entire studio

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.520
to work on this project, all at the same time.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.960
I'm like a task manager, and this is a multi-core processor

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.222
and I'm trying to thinking out which tasks should go where and like

00:00:43.222 --> 00:00:46.360
kind of thinking - if this task is going to take Clint three hours

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.260
and this task is going to take Adrian one hour, then...

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.660
how do I balance that out? How, what do I have Adrienne do for those next two hours?

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.000
It's been challenging

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.580
Doing these kind of video game videos is what I love doing. We're...

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.780
We're filmmakers that are very inspired by video games

00:00:58.780 --> 00:00:59.720
and if...

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.460
If we fail at this, we like - if we can't get this done in time, the team can't work together

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.279
Not only doesn't mean that we're not cut out to do what we want to do

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.560
It also means that

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.820
this whole growing the studio, and growing the team thing

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.280
may not be...working out as well as we planned

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:13.540
but I don't think it's going to happen

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.900
The channel is not going to get deleted. But then, that, that's the thing

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.920
It's like, we need to prove ourselves in this project.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.540
'Cos we're proving that as a team

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.440
We're gonna do good work and we're gonna do it fast and we're gonna do it good

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.260
I said that twice. I said good twice

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.060
Well!

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:26.200
[Laughs]

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.180
Carmichael, your'e correcting my grammar

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:28.780
[Laughs]

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.040
It's hard to focus on five different things. have you ever tried?

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.060
You only have two eyeballs, and they usually focus on one thing

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.400
[Laughs]

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.420
[Adrian:] Unfortunately...

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.320
we just don't have the same time that you do on a short film

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.960
where you might have like a week or two to do an entire color grid

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:43.300
I have a day and a half

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.700
What is color? What is color grading?

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.800
To me - it's the best part of the film making process

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.940
You basically have, like this bland looking thing

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.440
You're looking at something for so long. Looks so bland

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.760
Suddenly, putting on sunglasses that just saturate your image and it makes everything beautiful

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:04.140
Like, imagine seeing everything black and white your whole life and then something just putting on glasses

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.540
and everything just becomes...

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.160
colorful

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.020
That- that to me is what color is

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.840
It can create tone, mood....

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.000
You can create a unique look. You can use color as a storytelling device

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.100
Going into Da Vinci - building a LUT

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:19.100
Going back into Premiere - creating an adjustment layer

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.180
bringing in that LUT that you just created, onto the adjustment layer.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.480
Locking that layer and then going into individual clips to

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.620
tweak certain exposures. Change the ISO - 'cos we're still working with like the raw files

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:34.580
Is this the only way, or even the right way to do color correction?

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.660
Definitely not

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.160
But is it a quick way? And is it an efficient way?

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.620
I would say so.

