00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:55.440
being like, you only spent 15 minutes on this shot

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:56.860
I don't know how it's gonna look

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:57.760
and it's pretty solid

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.400
[Sam:] Thanks. I really appreciate that

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.200
[Wren:] Dude Red Giant--

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.780
[Sam:] Red Giant they got it all

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:03.000
they got all the best plugins

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:04.140
[Niko:] Tell you guys a seceret

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:05.260
I haven't done

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.260
visual effects. Well specifically gun visual effects

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.020
for like, over two years now

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:11.300
Ever since Wren joined

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.060
He's really alleviated a lot of the work off my plate

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.380
But it's time to come back

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:16.800
time to come out of retirement

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:18.300
for this shot right here

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:20.820
[Mouthing gun noises]

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.060
And I want to tell you some of the tips and tricks

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.060
and secrets to making this stuff look good

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:26.000
You'll notice in this shot

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.850
There's bullet hits that seem to have shading on them. They're casting shadows

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.410
They feel like they're connected to the environment

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.540
And if you've ever tried doing some these effects yourself - probably wondered why

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:35.060
when you start out, it doesn't look so good

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.080
You probably make your background footage. Drop it in

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.700
and you grab one of your ground hit elements that maybe you get from drop that in

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.580
Action VFX or Action Essentials

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.780
You drop that in. Oh, it needs transparency

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:46.060
So you put it on the screen mode or something

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:48.060
and you got yourself, cool bullet hit

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.060
and it looks really fake

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:51.760
Ehh...

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:52.760
Right?

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:53.740
Even if it's motion tracked

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.280
It doesn't look like it's supposed to be there, does it?

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:58.400
so I'm gonna show you a secret to gun effects and what I do to make this stuff look good.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.200
What I do to make it feel like it's part of the scene

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:04.579
Even if you're not doing visual effects, interesting to see how people create reality and bend reality with their shot

00:11:04.579 --> 00:11:07.459
It's like watching a painter sit down and paint a photo-real painting

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.700
So at the end of the day when it comes to making good visual effects. It's the same dicipline

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.440
You're going to be inventing something

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.920
with your tools and with your mind that looks real

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.000
so you need to know about real life and how to represent it and make it look real

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.200
If you can draw a good picture. If you can paint a nice picture

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:21.660
You can definitely do visual effects

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:24.240
First things first. I'll talk about these bullet hits here coming off this rock

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.940
So there's a lot of things you want to think about when making a bullet hit look good

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.060
You need a good element that you're going to drop in

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.930
Some of the best ones are either from Action Essentials

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.100
But they're really kind of old by now and used by everybody

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.860
Or action VFX. Which is a

00:11:35.860 --> 00:11:40.440
VFX stock footage company based in the UK and they're really putting on some really solid stuff

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:43.090
Once you drop the stuff in it's going to need work to make it look good

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.560
Here's some of the things to think about

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:48.420
lets say you just have your footage. Your background footage here and you drop in an element

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.720
In this case, I'm gonna grab the element. "Wall side hits 3"

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.640
Boom. It's in

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.080
Now, I did some motion tracking here. You can track points in After Effects.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.780
Or you can track little areas in Mocha

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.060
It's not very hard to track a point in a scene

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:04.059
So if you're going to have a bullet hit or an impact in your scene, need to track the point where it's going to hit

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:06.100
So it sticks there if your cameras moving. Basic stuff

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.960
But one of the first steps to making things look like the part of the environment

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:10.480
Secondly you have your element in there

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:11.830
It doesn't look real. It's in there right now

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:15.340
It's just like-- it looks like a piece of footage pasted on top. We got to start making it look real

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.760
In drawing, they talked about making a drawing on a piece of paper

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:20.020
You're connecting it to the page

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:22.540
and you connect something to the page with a shadow

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.940
So anytime you add elements, and you really want to feel like it's connected to the environment

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:27.460
You need to add a shadow

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:31.840
There we go. Suddenly. It feels like this dust hit, is actually there on top of the rock

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.500
It looks pretty solid. But you know what

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:36.100
still kind of flat. Like look at the dirt all around us here

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.460
There's all these different tonalities

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.480
there's lighting coming from a certain direction

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.480
and this dust hit, just it's kind of one color

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.000
even though it has some depth to it now there's a shadow there

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:46.880
It's just not reading. I'm gonna add another element. It's from the same pack

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.980
So I'm going to add "Wall side hit 11"

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:49.640
Wow

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:52.000
Here's the thing. So this element I added behind the other one

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.000
It's a slightly different color. It didn't come that way

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.619
I made it that way. You have basic ways to adjust your color

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:59.979
I just use hue and saturation, and I also added some levels to thicken

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.240
The effect. Because I didn't do levels on the color channels

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.200
I did levels on the Alpha channels

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.520
You can treat your alpha channels like a black and white image and increase the contrast

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:12.020
decrease the contrast. Brighten it, darken it. And it will affect your transparency accordingly

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:15.640
Now these two elements, layered together, feel a little bit more robust

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:16.840
It feels a little bit less--

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:21.649
Like I just pasted a piece of footage on my shot and like I'm actually getting an element crafted for the shot

00:13:21.649 --> 00:13:24.739
But you know what? It's still super flat. So this is my number one secret

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.140
This is the thing that I teach everybody when they come into Corridor and they start doing gun effects

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.440
I duplicate it again, and I draw a mass around

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.540
half of it, from the direction the light is coming from

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:37.040
and if I want to, I can also brighten it a little bit

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.220
This little trick of just duplicating it and masking it gives it volume and gives it shape

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.040
it actually starts making it look like it's being shaded from the light

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:47.280
and this is where a little bit of that drawing and painting like, know-how comes into play

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.140
So you think about like drawing a sphere on a piece of paper and you give it a light direction

00:13:50.149 --> 00:13:52.698
Oh, I'm gonna put the shadow over here. I'm gonna shade the sphere here

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:55.780
It's the same thing from these elements. You need to think about them existing in this world

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:57.080
Is this element be lit by the sun?

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.080
Yeah, probably - it's out there under the Sun

00:13:59.080 --> 00:13:59.699
[Laughs]

00:13:59.699 --> 00:14:02.329
Which direction would it be lit from? What would the colors be like?

00:14:02.329 --> 00:14:03.460
How bright would it be?

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:08.260
Not done yet. That little shading a bit looks great, until the smoke starts thinning out

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:11.680
'Cos once those-- the dust gets thinner, it shouldn't really be picking up a thick

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.020
Volumetric kind of look. So I just simply framed transparency

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:18.280
Starts at 100 goes down to about 20% and sits that through the rest of the shot. Nice

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.660
This dark element behind it, that kind of needed shadow too. So I added one of those as well

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.380
and to add a shadow...it's pretty simple

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:25.960
All I did to add a shadow, was take my element

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.460
duplicate it, blur it. Oh and I put on multiply

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:32.000
Now, shadows in real life could be different colors depending on the color of your ambient light

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:34.600
This case, I did just a slight shift back towards...

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.840
purplish oranges. Because blue sky tends to make your shadows blue

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.540
Or you can always look at the shadows in your scene - the real shadows, for a reference

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.320
All that together, pretty much makes it look like this...hit is part of the shot

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.020
there's one last thing I did

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:48.940
Last little bit of magic here is...

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:51.860
A little bit of a bullet hit...right there

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.520
I added some camera shake, actually as a second pass

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:55.740
For when the hit happens

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.820
Which helps make it feel like it's part of the scene. It also hides all my small mistakes

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:00.500
[Laughs]

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:04.100
A good VFX artist is always looking to the elements in their scene that are real

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:06.040
for reference to make sure your colors match

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:09.400
These elements will give you really good looking bullet impacts

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:10.160
If you can

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:13.560
Hit all those check marks and hit all those boxes. Turn everything back on here...

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.320
[Multiple gun shots]

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.270
[Character:] Sam!

